.Dd October 19, 2026
.Dt SCREEN-EXTEND 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm screen-extend
.Nd lay an external X11 output out as the primary, beside the internal panel
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl nv
.Op Fl i Ar internal
.Op Fl m Ar mode
.Op Fl o Ar output
.Op Fl p Ar place
.Nm
.Op Fl nv
.Op Fl i Ar internal
.Fl s
.Nm
.Fl h
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
extends an X11 desktop onto an external output, on
.Fx
and Linux alike.
The external output becomes the primary, at its preferred mode or at
.Fl m Ns 's,
at 0x0, and the internal panel is placed against it at its own
preferred mode: below it, above it, to its left or right, or off.
With no external output connected, or with
.Fl s ,
the internal panel alone is left on, as the primary.
So one command, run on docking and on undocking alike, gives the
layout for either.
.Pp
The internal panel is
.Fl i ,
else the first connected eDP, LVDS or DSI output.
The external output is
.Fl o ,
else the first other connected output that lists a mode: a port that
says connected but has no EDID has nothing to drive, and choosing it
would leave the real screen dark.
A dock that renumbers its ports
.Pq DP-1, DP-1-1, DP-3-1
therefore needs no editing.
Any other connected output is turned off, as is an output still driving
a mode after it was disconnected, which an undock leaves behind, so the
result never overlaps what was there before.
Positions are worked out and given explicitly, rather than with
.Ql --below
and the like, so the layout does not depend on the mode an output was
left in; outputs are aligned on their top or left edge.
.Pp
.Nm
runs at the moment a dock is plugged in or wakes up, when its outputs
are still appearing.
It therefore asks
.Xr xrandr 1
twice, a settling second apart
.Pq Ev SCREEN_EXTEND_SETTLE ,
and lays the outputs out from the second answer.
The whole layout is one
.Xr xrandr 1
call; afterwards it is read back and compared, output by output, and
applied again, up to three times, because a waking dock can drop the
request while
.Xr xrandr 1
still reports success.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width "-i internal"
.It Fl h
Print the usage and exit.
.It Fl i Ar internal
The internal panel, instead of the first connected eDP, LVDS or DSI
output.
It must be connected.
.It Fl m Ar mode
The external output's mode, as
.Ar W Ns x Ns Ar H ,
instead of its preferred one.
The output must list it.
.It Fl n
Dry run: print the
.Xr xrandr 1
command instead of running it.
The outputs are probed without the settling pause, and nothing is read
back.
.It Fl o Ar output
The external output, instead of the first other connected one that
lists a mode.
It must be connected, and must not be the internal panel.
.It Fl p Ar place
Where the internal panel goes:
.Cm below
.Pq the default ,
.Cm above ,
.Cm left ,
.Cm right ,
or
.Cm off .
Without an external output, the panel stays on whatever
.Ar place
says.
.It Fl s
Single: the internal panel alone, as the primary, even with an external
output connected; every other output is turned off.
It takes none of
.Fl m ,
.Fl o
and
.Fl p .
.It Fl v
Report the outputs found, the layout chosen and each attempt that did
not take, on stderr.
.El
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width SCREEN_EXTEND_SETTLE
.It Ev SCREEN_EXTEND_SETTLE
The seconds to wait between the two probes, and between attempts; 1
by default, more for a slower dock.
A number, with a fraction or not.
.It Ev TMPDIR
Where the probe is kept while
.Nm
runs, instead of
.Pa /tmp .
.It Ev XRANDR
The
.Xr xrandr 1
command to run, instead of
.Ql xrandr .
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It 0
The layout was applied, or printed with
.Fl n .
.It 1
No output is connected, an output named is not connected, a mode is
not listed,
.Fl m
was given with no external output to set,
.Fl s
was given with no internal panel connected, or the layout would not
stick after three attempts.
.It 2
A usage error, a
.Ar mode
not of the form
.Ar W Ns x Ns Ar H
a
.Ar place
not in the list, or
.Fl s
with
.Fl m ,
.Fl o
or
.Fl p
included.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
On docking or undocking, the external screen as the primary and the
laptop's panel below it, or the panel alone:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ screen-extend
.Ed
.Pp
The panel to the left of the external screen, which runs at 1920x1080:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ screen-extend -p left -m 1920x1080
.Ed
.Pp
The external screen alone, with what would be run shown first:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ screen-extend -n -p off
.Ed
.Pp
The laptop's panel alone, an external screen still plugged in or not:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ screen-extend -s
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr is-docked 1 ,
.Xr screen-mirror 1 ,
.Xr xrandr 1
.Sh CAVEATS
.Nm
works under X11 only; a Wayland session has no
.Xr xrandr 1
to drive.
